This is Zhi-Wei Sun's 2-4-6-8 Conjecture (OEIS A306477), an open research problem for which a $2,468 prize has been offered. It has been verified computationally up to $1.2 \times 10^{12}$ but no proof is known.

I have no proof of this conjecture. It is a genuine open problem in additive number theory. The four summands are tetrahedral-type binomial coefficients of increasing order:
- $\binom{w+2}{2}$ ranges over triangular numbers $\{1, 3, 6, 10, 15, \dots\}$
- $\binom{x+3}{4}$ ranges over 4-simplex (pentatope) numbers $\{1, 5, 15, 35, 70, \dots\}$
- $\binom{y+5}{6}$ ranges over 6-simplex numbers $\{1, 7, 28, 84, 210, \dots\}$
- $\binom{z+7}{8}$ ranges over 8-simplex numbers $\{1, 9, 45, 165, 495, \dots\}$

The conjecture asserts that every positive integer is a sum of one number from each of these four sequences. This is a deep problem in the spirit of Waring-type problems but with mixed-order figurate numbers, and it is beyond current techniques. I cannot produce a valid proof.

